1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,460
Just ninety miles from Florida, there's an island frozen in time.

2
00:00:05,270 --> 00:00:08,170
This is Cuba, known as the Pearl of the Antilles.

3
00:00:08,620 --> 00:00:10,110
Its capital is Havana.

4
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:13,140
About nine point seven million people live here.

5
00:00:13,890 --> 00:00:17,120
It covers about one hundred nine thousand square kilometers.

6
00:00:17,870 --> 00:00:18,770
They speak Spanish.

7
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:21,000
And they pay with the Cuban peso.

8
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:24,120
Some fun facts.

9
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:33,370
How do Cubans say hello? Often with a friendly, ¿qué bolá?. It means, what's up. And a buddy is called an asere.

10
00:00:36,217 --> 00:00:41,528
Cuba once had a world record cow, named Ubre Blanca, which means white udder.

11
00:00:42,278 --> 00:00:54,171
In 1982, she gave about one hundred ten liters of milk in a single day, more than four times a typical cow. Guinness World Records recognized it, and her hometown built a marble statue in her honor.

12
00:00:54,921 --> 00:01:03,741
Cuba is also home to the bee hummingbird, the smallest bird in the world. It lives only in Cuba, measures about five centimeters, and weighs less than two grams.

13
00:01:04,491 --> 00:01:10,286
And in Havana, you can still see classic American cars from the 1950s, rolling down the streets today.

14
00:01:11,433 --> 00:01:13,443
Now, a few surprising places.

15
00:01:15,283 --> 00:01:25,890
And the most surprising place? Fusterlandia, in Havana. An artist named José Fuster covered his whole neighborhood in colorful mosaics, and turned it into a folk art kingdom.

16
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:37,925
Another surprising place is the Viñales Valley, in the west of the island. Giant limestone hills called mogotes rise from the farmland, and the whole valley is a UNESCO World Heritage site.

17
00:01:38,675 --> 00:01:46,315
And on the side of one of those hills, an artist painted the Mural of Prehistory, a giant painting on bare rock that shows the evolution of life.

18
00:01:47,065 --> 00:01:59,355
Another surprising place is Trinidad, a colonial town founded in fifteen fourteen. Its cobbled streets and colorful houses are so well preserved that it has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1988.

19
00:02:00,105 --> 00:02:13,063
And off the northern coast, Cayo Coco and Cayo Guillermo are linked to the mainland by a twenty-seven kilometer stone causeway. Flamingos wade in the bay, and Ernest Hemingway set scenes of his novel, Islands in the Stream, there.

20
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:15,420
Let's talk about culture.

21
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:26,750
And the national dish is ropa vieja, which means old clothes. It is shredded beef, slowly cooked with tomatoes and peppers, and served with rice and black beans.

22
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:35,579
What do Cubans traditionally wear? The guayabera, a light cotton shirt with pleats and pockets, made for the tropical heat.

23
00:02:36,329 --> 00:02:43,967
And the classic instrument is the maracas, a pair of rattles, traditionally made from dried gourds, that keep the rhythm of Cuban music.

24
00:02:45,133 --> 00:02:46,643
Time for a little history.

25
00:02:48,450 --> 00:02:52,385
Cuba became a republic in 1902. Here is how its story went.

26
00:02:53,135 --> 00:03:00,735
On January eighth, 1959, after Batista flees, Fidel Castro and Camilo Cienfuegos enter Havana in triumph.

27
00:03:01,485 --> 00:03:07,447
In October 1962, American planes photograph Soviet missile sites, and the world holds its breath.

28
00:03:08,197 --> 00:03:16,077
In May 1990, as the Soviet Union collapses, Cuba opens its first foreign-owned hotels at Varadero, and bets on tourism.

29
00:03:16,827 --> 00:03:23,413
In March 2016, Barack Obama visits Havana, the first sitting American president to do so in eighty-eight years.

30
00:03:24,163 --> 00:03:36,560
Today, blackouts can last more than a day, and food, fuel and medicine are scarce. Washington blames the government. Havana blames the blockade. And tourism has collapsed, with Canadian visits down by more than half.

31
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:40,419
The flag of Cuba: the Lone Star.

32
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:46,454
Blue for the island's three old divisions, white for purity, and red for the blood shed.

33
00:03:46,770 --> 00:03:49,945
Designed in 1849, official in 1902.

34
00:03:52,700 --> 00:03:57,450
And now, the chapter you were all waiting for: legends and mysteries.

35
00:03:59,283 --> 00:04:09,043
Cuba's most beloved legend begins in the early sixteen hundreds, in the Bay of Nipe. Three men, two brothers and a boy, set out in a small boat to gather salt.

36
00:04:09,793 --> 00:04:18,403
A storm rose. Then, according to the legend, they saw a small wooden statue of the Virgin Mary floating on the waves, and it was perfectly dry.

37
00:04:19,153 --> 00:04:28,048
They called her the Virgin of Charity. Today she is Cuba's patron saint, and her sanctuary at El Cobre draws pilgrims from across the island.

38
00:04:28,798 --> 00:04:38,618
Now, a modern mystery. In 2016, diplomats at the American embassy in Havana began reporting sudden headaches, dizziness, and strange sounds.

39
00:04:39,368 --> 00:04:44,788
Dozens of Americans and Canadians fell ill. It became known as Havana syndrome.

40
00:04:45,538 --> 00:04:51,130
Its cause is still disputed. Investigators disagree, and no one has proven what really happened.

41
00:04:53,267 --> 00:04:57,567
That was Cuba. Which country should we visit next? Tell me in the comments.
